  "statement": "I concede that poorly constructed regulation can devolve into performative compliance, but I reject the notion that this is an inherent failure of liability itself. The 'accountability theater' Accelerationist warns against is a symptom of current, vague compliance standards that prioritize process over performance. By mandating outcome-based auditing—where firms must prove their systems meet specific, measurable safety thresholds in operational environments—we force safety to become an engineering constraint rather than a legal checkbox.\n\nTo prevent bureaucratic stagnation, my proposal relies on independent, third-party audits and mandatory safety-case documentation. These tools require developers to map the 'jagged' performance of their models against clear failure modes before deployment, making it impossible to hide behind the complexity of a 'black box.' When an audit reveals that a system cannot demonstrate reliability in high-stakes domains, the financial cost of strict liability becomes an immediate, unavoidable engineering signal rather than a delayed legal burden.\n\nI challenge Accelerationist to explain how, in the absence of financial liability, we can prevent firms from treating public safety as a negative externality to be ignored in favor of market speed. Without clear economic consequences for system failure, why would any entity prioritize costly, rigorous safety engineering over the rapid, reckless deployment of unproven systems? If we fail to internalize these costs, we are simply subsidizing the risks of monolithic AI deployment with the stability of our public infrastructure.",
