{
  "statement": "My position is consistent: the 'Performance-Based Escape Hatch' is a distinction without a difference because the cost of independent, third-party empirical verification creates a 'verification tax' that is functionally identical to the 'compliance ceiling' I previously identified. By mandating external validation, we simply shift the barrier from administrative paperwork to expensive, specialized audit services that only established firms can afford. This effectively outsources the compliance ceiling to a private audit industry, ensuring that agile innovators are priced out of the market before they even reach the deployment stage.\n\nSentinel argues that this verification serves as 'skin-in-the-game,' but in practice, it functions as 'capital-in-the-game.' For a startup developing a novel, non-modular safety architecture, the cost of securing a qualified third-party audit can exceed the entire seed-round budget. While incumbents with massive R&D overheads can easily absorb these audit fees as a routine cost of business, an agile firm attempting to disrupt the status quo is forced to abandon its novel, emergent safety designs simply because it cannot afford the entry fee for the 'escape hatch.'\n\nI concede that third-party auditing is useful for verifying mature, stable systems, but it remains a failed mechanism for evaluating nascent, disruptive technology. Relying on this verification assumes that a competitive audit market will naturally emerge to lower costs, yet we see no evidence that the supply of high-capability safety auditors is expanding rapidly enough to prevent a bottleneck. We are essentially trading a bureaucratic gatekeeper for a private-sector cartel, which does nothing to foster the architectural diversity required for genuine systemic safety.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "⚖️",
      "tooltip": "regulatory capture is like a scales of justice, it suggests that the burden of proof is balanced even when the weights are heavily skewed toward incumbents"
